From: Michael Bommarito <michael.bommarito@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 22 Apr 2026 12:31:19 -0400
Subject: [RFC PATCH 0/1] mm/kmsan: arch-opt-in shadow init hook to unblock new
 architectures

Hello Alexander, Dmitry, mm list,

This RFC proposes a small addition to mm/kmsan/ that lets
architectures reserve and map their KMSAN shadow/origin VA regions
at early boot without patching mm/kmsan/init.c. The patch is
deliberately minimal and non-invasive:

  - One new __weak hook (kmsan_arch_init_early_shadow) called
    from kmsan_init_shadow() before the existing reserved-range
    sweep. Default is an empty no-op.
  - One visibility change: kmsan_record_future_shadow_range()
    becomes non-static so an override can register arch-specific
    ranges through the same path the generic sweep already uses.
  - No existing call site changes; no policy in mm/kmsan/.

Architectures that don't define the weak override (x86_64, s390)
observe no behavioral difference. A local build verification on
x86_64 defconfig + CONFIG_KMSAN=y with LLVM=1 shows mm/kmsan/init.o
grows by 32 text bytes + 8 data bytes after the patch — the
irreducible cost of the weak hook + the new call site — with no
semantic change in the existing sweep.

Motivating user
===============

User-Mode Linux (UML), which is seeing renewed investment (backend
abstraction via struct um_backend_ops, section split for dynamic
instrumentation, full ftrace + kprobes + kretprobes via rethook +
function_graph, BPF JIT, KASAN / KFENCE / KCSAN / UBSAN per
profile, AFL-compatible forkserver for fuzz, a Rust uml-launcher
host tool, etc. — see Documentation/virt/uml/redesign/README.md in
the accompanying linux-um tree).

UML runs as a host userspace process. It has no direct-map region;
its KASAN shadow is a host mmap() at a fixed virtual address, set
up pre-main() by the arch-specific .kasan_init section pointer
(arch/um/kernel/mem.c::kasan_init() + arch/um/include/asm/kasan.h).
KMSAN's shadow and origin require the same mmap treatment AND
per-page metadata via struct page, which only the generic init
sweep can provide — hence the hook.

With the hook, UML defines kmsan_arch_init_early_shadow() in its
arch tree, mmaps the shadow/origin ranges at fixed VAs past the
KASAN shadow, and calls kmsan_record_future_shadow_range() for
each so the subsequent kmsan_init_alloc_meta_for_range() sweep
populates per-page metadata for them. The companion UML-side
series is separate from this one and only makes sense after this
lands; I'd be happy to post it alongside as a dependent series if
helpful.

Why RFC
=======

The semantic surface is small, but it IS a new surface on
mm/kmsan/. I'd rather have your approval on the shape before UML
depends on it — if a callback-registration API would fit better,
or if you'd prefer the hook live elsewhere in the init sequence,
I can revise. The diff at the proposed shape is ~20 lines of
generic code and I've tried to make every decision small and
defensible.

Payoff
======

Beyond the immediate UML use case, the hook is a path for any
future arch that wants to use a non-direct-map shadow layout
without carrying a patch to mm/kmsan/. UML as an existence proof
gives KMSAN a second, fast, host-process-native testing target
that doesn't require KVM or hardware — useful for regression-
testing the sanitizer itself, and for running KMSAN under UML's
syzkaller vm/uml backend (in flight) and AFL-compatible fork-
server (landed).

Checklist
=========

  - scripts/checkpatch.pl --strict: clean.
  - x86_64 defconfig + CONFIG_KMSAN=y + LLVM=1: mm/kmsan/init.o
    builds clean; semantic behavior of kmsan_init_shadow() is
    unchanged when no arch override is defined (verified by
    symbol table + size delta analysis).
  - Full x86_64 boot verification with CONFIG_KMSAN=y: pending
    (request for reviewer or I can arrange one pre-merge).
  - UML-side companion series: on uml-redesign-plan branch;
    boot + KMSAN KUnit results TBD once this hook lands.

Thank you for your time.

